But it could be balanced. Logically, if more than one beam is coming
out your wand, it should use up more charges. Either that, or each
beam should only have 1/x the power of the original, where x is the
number of rays produced.
I like the idea of the 10% chance of the gem exploding (producing
dangerous flying debris that could hit you), or maybe 10% chance of
one of the beams hitting *you* by accident.
Of course if you wanted to set this up you'd need some way to tell how
many rays are going to come out of the gem. Perhaps an uncut gem
(default) would yield zero rays, and for each facet you cut in the
gem, an additional ray is produced (giving up to 9)? Of course then
you would need equipment for cutting the gems. Perhaps a crysknife?
This would give some purpose to picking up and converting worm teeth
to crysknives (which I never bother to do anymore.) Also, every time
you try to cut a facet in the gem, it could have a chance of
shattering (unless you are an Archaeologist or have Expert skill with
knife), increasing with the number of facets already present.
Another thought is that certain colors of gems would only be
compatible with certain wands. If you tried to use the wrong color of
gem with the wrong wand, the gem would have a much higher chance of
shattering. For example, rubies (red) could only be used with /oF,
sapphires (blue) with /oC, and so forth. Also, only hard gems should
be usuable -- soft gems would be unable to channel the power required,
and would disintegrate if you tried to use them.
Any other suggestions? This idea actually really appeals to me,
because after you run out of stores to sell them at, gems are pretty
well worthless (except for engraving). This would give you a good
reason to carry them around.

